window.docContents[116].resolve({"/Maps/#section-vertex-transitivity-of-mycielskians":{"contents":"The Mycielskian of a graph is rarely vertex-transitive. In fact we can see this\neasily by looking at degrees, which are equal in any vertex-transitive graph\n(IsVertexTransitive.exists_isRegularOfDegree). If V is finite and nonempty then\nmμ[G] is vertex-transitive iff G is isomorphic to K[2].\n\nTo help in the proof we first construct a graph isomorphism from mμ[K[2]] to\nC[5], which we already know to be vertex-transitive. We define both maps using\nMathlib's vector notation: ![a, b, c] is the function Fin 3 → α sending 0,\n1 and 2 to a, b and c. So the forward map sends the apex none to 0,\n0 ₗ and 1 ₗ to 3 and 2, and 0 ᵣ and 1 ᵣ to 1 and 4.\n\nLeft: K_2. Right: its Mycielskian drawn as a 5-cycle. The orange numbers are the vertices of C_5 to which mycielskianCompleteTwoIsoCycleFive sends them\n\nA graph isomorphism from mμ[K[2]] to C[5]./-- A graph isomorphism from mμ[K[2]] to C[5]. -/\ndef mycielskianCompleteTwoIsoCycleFive : mμ[K[2]] ≃g C[5] where\n  toFun := Option.elim' 0 (Sum.elim ![3, 2] ![1, 4])\n  invFun := ![none, 0 ᵣ, 1 ₗ, 0 ₗ, 1 ᵣ]\n  left_inv := by decide +revert\n  right_inv := by decide +revert\n  map_rel_iff' := by decide +revert\n\n\nThe forward direction only needs mμ[G] to be regular, so we isolate it as a lemma:\ncomparing the degrees of u ₗ, u ᵣ and none shows that if V is finite and\nnonempty and mμ[G] is regular then G is a single edge.\n\nlemma nonempty_iso_completeGraph_two_of_regular_mycielskian {V : Type*}\n    {G : SimpleGraph V} [Finite V] [Nonempty V] [mμ[G].LocallyFinite] {d : ℕ}\n    (hd : mμ[G].IsRegularOfDegree d) : Nonempty (G ≃g K[2]) := by\n  classical\n  obtain ⟨v : V⟩ := ‹Nonempty V›\n  cases nonempty_fintype V\n  -- From d = 2 * G.degree u and d = G.degree u + 1 we get G.degree u = 1.\n  have h1 : G.IsRegularOfDegree 1 := fun u => by\n    have hₗ : mμ[G].degree u ₗ = 2 * G.degree u := by simp\n    have hᵣ : mμ[G].degree u ᵣ = G.degree u + 1 := by simp\n    grind [hd u ₗ, hd u ᵣ]\n  -- Then d = Fintype.card V forces Fintype.card V = 2, as required.\n  have hcard : Fintype.card V = 2 := by\n    have h₀ : mμ[G].degree none = Fintype.card V := by simp\n    have hᵣ : mμ[G].degree v ᵣ = G.degree v + 1 := by simp\n    grind [hd none, hd v ᵣ, h1 v]\n  -- we now use the fact that if G is regular of degree Fintype.card V - 1\n  -- then G is complete.\n  obtain rfl : G = ⊤ :=\n    G.isRegularOfDegree_card_sub_one_iff_eq_top.1 (by simpa [hcard] using h1)\n  exact ⟨Iso.completeGraph (Fintype.equivFinOfCardEq hcard)⟩\n\n\nThe Mycielskian of a graph on a finite nonempty vertex type is vertex-transitive\niff the original graph is isomorphic to K[2]./-- The Mycielskian of a graph on a finite nonempty vertex type is vertex-transitive\niff the original graph is isomorphic to K[2]. -/\ntheorem isVertexTransitive_mycielskian_iff {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) [Finite V]\n    [Nonempty V] : mμ[G].IsVertexTransitive ↔ Nonempty (G ≃g K[2]) := by\n  classical\n  constructor\n  · intro h\n    cases nonempty_fintype V\n    obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := IsVertexTransitive.exists_isRegularOfDegree h\n    exact nonempty_iso_completeGraph_two_of_regular_mycielskian hd\n  · intro ⟨e⟩\n    exact e.mapMycielskian.symm.comp mycielskianCompleteTwoIsoCycleFive.symm\n      |>.isVertexTransitive (isVertexTransitive_cycleGraph _)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Automorphisms","header":"5.6.3. Vertex-Transitivity of Mycielskians","id":"/Maps/#section-vertex-transitivity-of-mycielskians"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-top-and-bottom":{"contents":"The complete graph ⊤ has all possible edges (every pair of distinct vertices is\nadjacent), while the empty graph ⊥ has no edges. These are the greatest and least\nelements of the subgraph order. Although completeGraph V and emptyGraph V are\nabbreviations for ⊤ and ⊥, we generally use top and bot in naming results\nabout these graphs.\n\n#check top_adj  -- (⊤ : SimpleGraph V).Adj v w ↔ v ≠ w\n#check bot_adj  -- (⊥ : SimpleGraph V).Adj v w ↔ False\n\n\nEvery graph is a subgraph of the complete graph.\n\nexample : G ≤ ⊤ := le_top\n\n\nThe empty graph is a subgraph of every graph.\n\nexample : ⊥ ≤ G := bot_le\n\n\nIf G is a proper subgraph of H then G is a proper subgraph of the complete\ngraph.\n\nexample {G H : SimpleGraph V} (h : G < H) : G < ⊤ := lt_top_of_lt h\n\nend order\nend basics\n\n\nNotice that the last few proofs all made use of results about orders rather than\nanything specific to graphs. They all follow easily from Mathlib's instance of\nCompleteAtomicBooleanAlgebra (SimpleGraph V), which we displayed with #synth at\nthe start of this section.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph\u0009Order","header":"2.6.1. Top and Bottom","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-top-and-bottom"}});